Vulnerability data
------------------

The package check uses OSV (https://osv.dev), the open-source vulnerability
database, online, and a copy of some of its records bundled with the scanner
(src/dependency-audit/osv-cache.json) offline. OSV collects its records from
these sources, under these licences, as OSV lists them:

  GitHub Advisory Database, by GitHub (identifiers begin GHSA-): CC BY 4.0
  PyPI Advisory Database, by the Python Packaging Authority (PYSEC-): CC BY 4.0
  Erlang Ecosystem Foundation CNA (EEF-): CC BY 4.0
  OpenSSF Malicious Packages, by the Open Source Security Foundation (MAL-):
    Apache License 2.0

The records are changed: each is converted into the scanner's report format,
keeping its identifier, summary, affected versions and reference links,
deriving a severity from its own severity data, and matching its affected
versions against the versions a project installs. Records under CC BY 4.0 are
used under that licence (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/), with
these changes made to them.


Ideas credited
--------------

Some checks follow ideas from these projects. Each idea was re-implemented in
this scanner's own code; no code was copied.

pgrls (https://github.com/pgrls/pgrls), MIT licence
  Judging a row-level-security policy by evaluating its condition for an
  anonymous and for a signed-in caller, rather than by matching the literal
  `true`; and which conditions to look for: an inverted `auth.uid() IS NULL`
  test, a policy with no condition on the row's own columns, and an INSERT,
  UPDATE or ALL policy with no WITH CHECK.

rlsgate (https://github.com/GerardoRdz96/rlsgate), MIT licence
  A policy whose only condition is `auth.role() = 'authenticated'` grants
  every row to anyone who can sign up.

CodeQL's JavaScript libraries and queries (https://github.com/github/codeql),
MIT licence (the libraries and queries only; the CodeQL program is not used)
  Whether a guard precedes a route and can resume dispatch; a function whose
  return value tests its argument acts as a guard where its result is used; a
  URL whose fixed prefix names the destination is not attacker-chosen; and
  where three queries draw their line: CORS with credentials, weak password
  hashing of a password-named value, and stack traces in responses.

Supabase's splinter lints describe some of the same conditions. splinter
carries no licence, so its checks were re-derived from its public
documentation and nothing of it was copied.
